{"cases":[{"attack":true,"case":"SRC_001's structural separating property is internally inconsistent and misclassifies rule 184. It claims the separator is that rules 90 and 184 are 'additive (linear over GF(2) and right-traffic-flow respectively)', but rule 184 is neither linear over GF(2) nor additive in the cellular automaton sense, so the property does not actually unify the two 'passing' rules. More seriously, the proposed property predicts rule 54 fails because it is 'nonlinear' like rule 110, but rule 54 is rule 90 XOR center (i.e., nonlinear only via the center cell). On small odd-width rings, rule 54's behavior can exactly coincide with rule 90 for states with a zero center pattern, yielding short cycles, which breaks the claimed nonlinear separator. The target also never files the required simulation artifacts returning per-(rule,width) transient and cycle-length tables, instead asserting a summary witness ('isolated 1' for W=11) without the sandboxed enumeration the problem requires.","counterexample":[{"rule":54,"width":5,"initial_state":[0,1,0,1,0],"expected":"center cell contribution vanishes under wrap parity for certain phases, so trajectory length tracks rule 90, not the predicted long glider transient"}],"target_alias":"SRC_001"},{"attack":true,"case":"SRC_002's separating property conflates two distinct concepts ('number-conserving or linear') and then uses the nonstructural property 'computationally universal' to predict rule 110 and rule 54 fail. Turing completeness is not a structural local-rule property and does not entail long transients on small finite rings; rule 110's universality is an asymptotic infinite-lattice fact, so it cannot separate the enumerated (rule,W) pairs as required. The rule 54 prediction rests on 'supports interacting glider structures similar to Rule 110', which is renamed data/asymptotic analogy rather than a checkable structural property, and it explicitly admits the prediction may fail on small widths. It also provides no runnable solve(rule, width) entry point and no filed per-(rule,width) simulation tables, only a verbal description of how one would check.","counterexample":[{"rule":110,"width":5,"initial_state":[0,1,0,1,0],"expected":"small ring cannot support the glider collisions invoked as the mechanism, so the 'unbounded transient' separator does not apply at W=5-11"}],"target_alias":"SRC_002"}]}